      "text": "To find the number of distinct prime factors of the sum of the positive divisors of $400$, first list all its proper divisors:\n$(1, 2, 4, 5, 8, 10, 16, 20, 25, 40, 50, 80, 100, 125, 200, 250, 400)$.\nNext, compute the sum of these divisors by writing the product of their prime factorizations and then multiplying this by the result of $(1-3^{-1})(1-5^{-1})(1-7^{-1})$:\n$$$$\\left(\\left(1+2+4+10+20+40+50+200+400\\right) \\times \\left(1+5+25+125\\right) \\times \\left(1+7+49\\right) \\right) =107190.$$\nFinally, factorize $107190$. The resulting prime factors are $2, 3, 5, 7, 13, 31$, which give $6$ distinct prime factors.\n\nTherefore, the sum of the positive divisors of $400$ has $\\boxed{6}$ distinct prime factors.",
