    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To find the sum of the positive divisors of 400, we can first find the prime factorization of 400 and then use the formula for finding the sum of divisors.\n\nThe prime factorization of 400 is $2^4 \\cdot 5^2$.\n\nThe sum of the divisors of a number $n = p_1^{a_1} \\cdot p_2^{a_2} \\cdot \\cdots \\cdot p_k^{a_k}$ is given by $(1 + p_1 + p_1^2 + \\cdots + p_1^{a_1})(1 + p_2 + p_2^2 + \\cdots + p_2^{a_2}) \\cdots (1 + p_k + p_k^2 + \\cdots + p_k^{a_k})$.\n\nUsing this formula, we have:\n\n$400 = 2^4 \\cdot 5^2$, so\n\nDivisors = $(1 + 2 + 2^2 + 2^3 + 2^4)(1 + 5 + 5^2)$\n\nFactoring, we get:\n\nDivisors = $(1 - 2)(1 - 2^2)(1 - 2^3)(1 - 2^4)(1^1 - 5)(1^1 - 5^2)$\n\nDivisors = $-1 \\cdot -3 \\cdot -7 \\cdot -15 \\cdot 1 - 31$\n\nDivisors = $(1 + 2 + 4 + 8 + 16)(1 + 5 + 25)$ Divisors = $31 \\cdot 31$ Divisors = $31^2$\n\nSince all prime factors of 31^2 are distinct, the sum of the positive divisors of 400 has $\\boxed{2}$ distinct prime factors. Each factor multiplies 31 twice.",
